---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const highlights = [
  { title: 'Battles in rounds', text: 'Units have hit points and fight round by round. No more Triremes sinking Battleships.' },
  { title: 'Units heal', text: 'Every turn, in cities, fortified on land, or resting near the coast.' },
  { title: 'Railroads that work', text: 'Cities get railroads, and trains keep their moves through them.' },
  { title: 'Saves and autosaves', text: '10 save slots instead of 4, and 20 autosaves, one every turn instead of every 1000 years.' },
  { title: 'Fixed demographics', text: 'No more negative numbers in large empires.' },
  { title: 'Better random numbers', text: 'A modern generator instead of the weak one of 1991.' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Civ 1.5 Remastered"
  description="A C# remaster of Civilization (1991) for DOS: the original game, with better battles, healing, railroads and many fixes."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <h1>Civ 1.5 <span>Remastered</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Civilization from 1991, running natively on Windows. The same game you remember, with the rough edges fixed
        and a few things it always needed.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/get-started/`}>Get started</a>
        <a class="button" href={`${base}/whats-new/`}>What's new</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Screenshot name="title" caption="The title screen." />
  </section>

  <h2>Highlights</h2>
  <ul class="highlights">
    {
      highlights.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{item.title}</strong>
          <span>{item.text}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p><a href={`${base}/whats-new/`}>Everything that changed →</a></p>

  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>
    The original <code>CIV.EXE</code> was translated function by function into C#. The game logic is the original
    one, so the game plays like it did in 1991, but it runs as a modern .NET application in its own window, with the
    original AdLib and Roland music.
  </p>
  <p>
    You need your own copy of Civilization for DOS: the game reads its pictures, texts and data from the original
    files, which are not part of this project.
  </p>
</Layout>
